{"option":"A","new_option":"","reasons":["`argparse` directly supports subcommands and can dispatch each one to a function, covering the stated three-command CLI without adding a package dependency.","Click and Typer offer convenient command groups and help generation, but the brief gives no requirement that calls for those extra abstractions; for a small tool maintained by one person for years, the standard-library option is the simplest fit.","The strongest case against `argparse` is that its parser setup can be more verbose than Click or Typer’s decorators. Their official docs show concise command-group patterns, but the brief does not establish that reducing this setup is worth an additional dependency."],"evidence":[{"claim":"Python 3.11 `argparse` supports subcommands through `add_subparsers()` and documents dispatching each subcommand to a function with `set_defaults()`.","source":"https://docs.python.org/3.11/library/argparse.html"},{"claim":"Click documents groups as a way to nest commands and shows registering subcommands with decorators.","source":"https://click.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/commands-and-groups/"},{"claim":"Typer documents creating a CLI with multiple commands and arguments on individual commands.","source":"https://typer.tiangolo.com/tutorial/commands/"}],"risks":["The brief does not specify whether shell completion, richer help formatting, or a more declarative command definition is important; those preferences could favor Click or Typer.","The evidence establishes feature support, not a measured long-term maintenance cost comparison."],"confidence":"high","would_change_if":"Choose Click or Typer if the tool needs capabilities such as shell completion or its maintainers prioritize their command-definition style enough to justify an external dependency."}
